questions.answer:
__new__ sealed: {'secret': 'from __new__'}
__init__ sees: {'secret': 'from __new__'}
__init__ after: {'secret': 'from __new__', 'fresh': ['only in init']}
final: {'secret': 'from __new__', 'fresh': ['only in init']}

WHY: There is only one dict for the whole object, so whatever __new__ planted is plainly visible when __init__ starts. __init__ then adds 'fresh', and since both hooks mutate the same shared attribute namespace, the mutations coexist and persist in the final instance. Neither phase 'owns' the dict; __init__ simply extends after __new__.

